# Line endings. The repository stores LF and checks out LF, on every machine.
#
# The host here is Windows with core.autocrlf=true, which rewrites LF to CRLF on
# checkout. Without this file the bytes on disk become a property of *which clone
# you are standing in* rather than of the content, and this app has two places
# where that matters:
#
#   1. tools/stamp-sw.mjs fingerprints every precached file to derive the service
#      worker's cache name. It already normalises line endings before hashing, and
#      the comment there explains why — a fresh clone would otherwise compute a
#      different stamp and test/precache.test.js would fail with a message telling
#      you to restamp, which restamping does not fix. This file removes the cause
#      rather than leaving that defence carrying the whole weight alone.
#   2. tools/release.ps1 uploads the working tree as it stands. A CRLF checkout
#      would publish files that are functionally identical to the live ones but
#      byte-for-byte different, so every file would look changed on a deploy that
#      changed nothing.
#
# It also silences the "LF will be replaced by CRLF" warning on every git add.

* text=auto eol=lf

# Binaries, declared rather than guessed. Git's heuristic already detects these
# correctly, but a normalise applied to a woff2 or a png corrupts it, and that is
# not a failure worth leaving to detection.
*.woff2 binary
*.png    binary
*.ico    binary
*.tgz    binary

# Diff and merge hints. These are the files a diff is most often read on, and
# marking their language gives git better hunk headers.
*.js     diff=javascript
*.mjs    diff=javascript
*.css    diff=css
*.html   diff=html
*.py     diff=python
*.md     diff=markdown
